\[AD \le AB + BC + CD = 4 + 5 + 6 = 15.\]By Pythagoras on right triangle $ADE,$
\[9^2 + (\sqrt{16 - x^2} + \sqrt{25 - y^2} + \sqrt{36 - z^2})^2 = AD^2 \le 15^2,\]so $(\sqrt{16 - x^2} + \sqrt{25 - y^2} + \sqrt{36 - z^2})^2 \le 15^2 - 9^2 = 144.$  Hence,
\[\sqrt{16 - x^2} + \sqrt{25 - y^2} + \sqrt{36 - z^2} \le 14.\]Equality occurs when $x = \frac{14}{5},$ $y = 3,$ and $z = \frac{18}{5}.$  (Note that this corresponds to the case where $A,$ $B,$ $C,$ and $D$ are collinear.)  Thus, the maximum value we seek is $\boxed{14}.$

