Without loss of generality, assume that $F=(0,3).$ Then the second ellipse must be tangent to the first ellipse at the point $(0, 5).$ The sum of the distances from $(0,5)$ to the foci of the second ellipse is $3 + 5 = 7,$ so the length of the major axis of the second ellipse is $7.$ Since the distance between the foci of the second ellipse is $3,$ the length of the minor axis of the second ellipse is \[\sqrt{7^3-3^3} = \boxed{3\sqrt{10}}.\]
